#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147293 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6523c3be-22c9-5b57-985f-1af4fa4a3cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Instances of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "7887c216-958f-5dfe-8889-b4c3033fbafe" :question/math-academy-id "e-6624" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n- $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n\n- $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n\n- $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2,$ $b=6,$ $c=-5,$ and $f(x)=0.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2.$ Instead, it is multiplied by an $x.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147294 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cb1a475-4f73-531d-b14c-37000cfc37ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Instance of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "08304289-e9fc-5914-a6e7-187ea5a40031" :question/math-academy-id "e-6625" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=t^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+8t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+8\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ 3{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(3\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -2$ and $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={c}_{1}{t}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147295 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10cb8c3a-0764-5082-b431-7f97b8bc5782" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "d86f4d20-8ebe-52ea-bf49-f0cdcd0a2600" :question/math-academy-id "e-6626" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0,  \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad  c_2 = -c_1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{4}}+2{c}_{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =-3{c}_{1}+2(-{c}_{1}) \\\\ 5 & =-5{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6523c3be-22c9-5b57-985f-1af4fa4a3cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Instances of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6a3398-cfaa-5f0d-85d9-7071da4bd6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy^{″} + 4xy^{′} + 7y = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146810} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3fe262-e221-5601-a0a9-649cf6d90905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 6xy^{′} + 9x^{2}y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146811} {:answer/id #uuid "ad87c5ab-3ea5-5a77-92b7-70a97008b03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2x^{2}y^{′} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146812} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab23060-ad14-5261-b4a3-0fda78161658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} + 2xy^{′} + 4y = \\frac{2}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146813} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab5e81d-9fb8-52c0-8987-9f4a03937761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 9y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab23060-ad14-5261-b4a3-0fda78161658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} + 2xy^{′} + 4y = \\frac{2}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146813} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f438a8f-b8f8-5450-bf9f-bfc845118b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7e398da-afcf-578c-b016-2a95047aca8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64714" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" :question/worked-solution "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}y′′+bxy′+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6523c3be-22c9-5b57-985f-1af4fa4a3cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Instances of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c68d94d-f544-57c6-9188-c44f2c44f11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-{t}^{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360910} {:answer/id #uuid "469910c6-3964-50b0-81d6-0c74402f8ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}-2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360911} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0f72ea-4cb9-5d5b-80e7-6a606012f048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360912} {:answer/id #uuid "11336ca3-2da3-58a2-85e1-eb3b2eca8443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-3y=4{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360913} {:answer/id #uuid "70e32d47-5b12-572c-b109-5d294c3d6d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}-3{t}^{2}y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0f72ea-4cb9-5d5b-80e7-6a606012f048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360912} :answer-field/id #uuid "624c4e2b-8940-5ce4-b07f-6a0b35ccb4c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0af108b7-6c19-599e-bfad-892954501ac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64780" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" :question/worked-solution "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+bt{y}^{′}+cy=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}-3y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6523c3be-22c9-5b57-985f-1af4fa4a3cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Instances of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a1612b8-0eab-5af5-955c-5f134ecf5102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{″}-2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360917} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fe40dc-a4b5-5d10-82ac-93aa0a37b6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360918} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3957a0-3032-585d-a5b0-150672218372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+xy=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360919} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe93780-cc92-5daa-9baa-db7e269f6f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y={x}^{3}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360920} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d7b750-d02f-5584-9cf8-d9d3175a4767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+4{x}^{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbe93780-cc92-5daa-9baa-db7e269f6f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y={x}^{3}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360920} :answer-field/id #uuid "d680977c-b9f2-5de1-acbc-e58727962301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3feea83a-4a10-529c-87d2-50fab516979e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64781" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" :question/worked-solution "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+bx{y}^{′}+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y={x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cb1a475-4f73-531d-b14c-37000cfc37ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Instance of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e6b0d81-f6d1-5734-97bb-42f97dc3cd88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360924} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecc2869-f9e1-5173-ac74-68b429cfa113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360925} {:answer/id #uuid "2aed8dc2-1b57-525e-b179-1e999fc2db2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360926} {:answer/id #uuid "338e341b-b05f-5e50-8677-dd5a18828f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360927} {:answer/id #uuid "c14c63e4-1233-5ad5-8300-c6ed0e1f27c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14c63e4-1233-5ad5-8300-c6ed0e1f27c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360928} :answer-field/id #uuid "a279ecf1-6871-5896-8878-30ba626c6891" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73c50c67-0e36-56ac-9c4c-739cefc62453" :question/math-academy-id "q-64791" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+4x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+4\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda +2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cb1a475-4f73-531d-b14c-37000cfc37ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Instance of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897dfbcc-99f3-51b4-9d08-2a67866b90e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{2} + c_{2}t^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146834} {:answer/id #uuid "923d63a8-b33c-5aa6-bed3-9bef12b810bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1} + c_{2}t^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146835} {:answer/id #uuid "b84dcc21-9de4-5972-bd52-95bb0fdd7c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{-3} + c_{2}t^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146836} {:answer/id #uuid "18850531-390c-5ee4-896e-805f4ffa9a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{2} + c_{2}t^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146837} {:answer/id #uuid "6c684eb2-61ac-50f4-93cb-a2cd438b388e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{2} + c_{2}t^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18850531-390c-5ee4-896e-805f4ffa9a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{2} + c_{2}t^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146837} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce2d2a5-35d9-5e66-8012-f8d04e4a461a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0f78c40-43f4-5bba-8222-db99c2fa4af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64803" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cb1a475-4f73-531d-b14c-37000cfc37ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Instance of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b566ddec-4121-5a50-9701-c083b6fdaa41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360931} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1c5eff-a8ec-5f38-ac60-b7adc95d6223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360932} {:answer/id #uuid "4b208c15-8436-5180-a9d9-766f6abab824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/4}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360933} {:answer/id #uuid "314b6e8d-5696-5acc-8ef0-182bf9dade7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360934} {:answer/id #uuid "2b799afd-27ac-5262-9fc4-02ffd078f7c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b799afd-27ac-5262-9fc4-02ffd078f7c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360935} :answer-field/id #uuid "8024343a-4866-59ff-b62a-467e99afcb48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec268263-da66-51b5-bc9f-102a4e291a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-64896" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+4x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+4\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda -1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda +1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10cb8c3a-0764-5082-b431-7f97b8bc5782" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487c9524-d262-51d6-8ee6-cd997fe38634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{2}-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360938} {:answer/id #uuid "647b6f1a-44b0-5660-8ef3-c59e7b8dd5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360939} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f63c55-2126-5b0e-9388-0596c0c7dc4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{3}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360940} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a2babe-b8e4-5154-8fdf-f2d1d6d91a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{3}-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360941} {:answer/id #uuid "326b2d4f-6ead-51ea-810c-6d6ba62ab987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1a2babe-b8e4-5154-8fdf-f2d1d6d91a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{3}-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360941} :answer-field/id #uuid "8876d5d6-a93b-54f4-be37-2d4055e34e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcdbfd86-d02b-511c-99b2-3cd68a8b17f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64897" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=8,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =3.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(2)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0=2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=8.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}(2)^{2} \\\\ 8 & ={c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =-4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =8{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{3}-4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6523c3be-22c9-5b57-985f-1af4fa4a3cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Instances of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca6c02f3-6692-5810-82c1-db73bc8177fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-{t}^{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360945} {:answer/id #uuid "18a314f0-0f8b-5c82-9f1e-1d24305c65f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+2{t}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360946} {:answer/id #uuid "3921f081-1354-5121-8234-18bf3e0cdd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-t{y}^{′}+6y=\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} {:answer/id #uuid "6b44103a-d964-5631-87c9-204774d9fa55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{y}^{″}+{t}^{2}{y}^{′}-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360948} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3f7e58-6fff-5b69-9ceb-cf373a8fdb18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-3{t}^{2}y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3921f081-1354-5121-8234-18bf3e0cdd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-t{y}^{′}+6y=\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} :answer-field/id #uuid "99158ad9-77e7-5022-8073-ce2d22f8b6df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d7d989c-dae6-54bf-a4b5-d19b2c4cba1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-93844" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" :question/worked-solution "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{t}^{2}y′′+bty′+cy=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n3{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-t{y}^{′}+6y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6523c3be-22c9-5b57-985f-1af4fa4a3cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Instances of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96d4d869-466d-5518-a0f3-18880176185e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5t{y}^{′}-{t}^{2}y=t+{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360952} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0948c-aece-5463-9d9c-e37bc97d6768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-{t}^{2}{y}^{′}+6y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360953} {:answer/id #uuid "d04f3d25-92c0-5293-9d54-46decf623365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t{y}^{″}-{t}^{2}{y}^{′}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360954} {:answer/id #uuid "fe278601-c172-5d03-a72d-7d71ba8b493f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-2y=t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360955} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3af006-2a94-5dd9-8f40-734467d37fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+{t}^{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe278601-c172-5d03-a72d-7d71ba8b493f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-2y=t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360955} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3fce4c0-1980-5d87-85aa-051d1e062764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1ea569a-45e0-5b4c-8ef2-ca47809c9cb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-93847" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" :question/worked-solution "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{t}^{2}y′′+bty′+cy=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n4{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-2y=t+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cb1a475-4f73-531d-b14c-37000cfc37ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Instance of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bba0fc54-ef76-5bb7-816a-74c4da6d292f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360959} {:answer/id #uuid "05425493-19af-531a-a975-2ba23f79e36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360960} {:answer/id #uuid "4ace7814-d3a4-57e6-bae3-ccc2a4a7ef67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{3}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360961} {:answer/id #uuid "3da17261-70cb-5b91-a364-5bdbb040d169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{3/4}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360962} {:answer/id #uuid "21e3b0ad-b30a-5857-9c79-2f974266a39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e3b0ad-b30a-5857-9c79-2f974266a39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360963} :answer-field/id #uuid "cec47a3c-bd2c-52fb-83f8-25a9197879cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4290ec71-e89b-51ac-aae2-37403c147613" :question/math-academy-id "q-93849" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+t{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(4\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{4}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cb1a475-4f73-531d-b14c-37000cfc37ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Instance of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a8d1e0-decb-53ae-84ca-37e6f739cfb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360966} {:answer/id #uuid "d1310d2f-b63e-512b-952e-343c7ed73af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360967} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc6f852-7827-57ff-8c8d-3b0826115c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360968} {:answer/id #uuid "080067d6-fedd-5e63-84e4-12c1404276b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360969} {:answer/id #uuid "38fdc3b4-92e2-5743-91f2-eb0925394800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38fdc3b4-92e2-5743-91f2-eb0925394800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360970} :answer-field/id #uuid "05875801-4fa0-571b-94ca-d71d0a4ad63c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1d233f9-3319-5285-9917-972dd3a72bc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-93850" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-3y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+7x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+7\\lambda -3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+7\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(2\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10cb8c3a-0764-5082-b431-7f97b8bc5782" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e39ad00-8c58-5ad7-9192-de7984316ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360973} {:answer/id #uuid "b468971e-3c1b-50e0-bf71-985c6d513768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+4\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360974} {:answer/id #uuid "d3465f90-351a-5962-9a73-85f57682177d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360975} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c6cf96-5a9a-582b-a84b-ac395bfc1a39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360976} {:answer/id #uuid "5200e911-f9ec-5cdd-af60-d33cf1ae9f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3465f90-351a-5962-9a73-85f57682177d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360975} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eec3813-8469-5332-b1ef-01e4593faf51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c979dba-bb29-55c5-b3a4-f9de892fa6e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-93855" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}\\sqrt{x},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{c}_{1}x+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ and ${c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 5 & =2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1-{c}_{1}}{2} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{3{c}_{1}+1}{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =3\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=3{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10cb8c3a-0764-5082-b431-7f97b8bc5782" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa7e609-86f6-5f82-ac69-31ce6c6bc088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=6\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360980} {:answer/id #uuid "2458380b-8adc-53ab-8317-5084d0bc922f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=-4t-2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360981} {:answer/id #uuid "cf72b731-614e-5adb-85c6-a989bc16536e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360982} {:answer/id #uuid "faefa58d-471e-59b4-93e7-f3a02a5497d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2t-3\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360983} {:answer/id #uuid "2805512d-8ca8-5295-b3b8-1612bf087b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=-5t-3\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0aa7e609-86f6-5f82-ac69-31ce6c6bc088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=6\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360980} :answer-field/id #uuid "881f1b5e-a8dc-5d67-a1d6-6fceca9cdd6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41b0b395-aafa-5a6d-a331-f5a55ec4e31d" :question/math-academy-id "q-93856" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=1-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}\\sqrt{t}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & ={c}_{1}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 4 & =1-{c}_{2}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{{c}_{2}+2}{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =6\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=6\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-5t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10cb8c3a-0764-5082-b431-7f97b8bc5782" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eca77ea1-d7dc-5914-80d6-45b512fd7341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360987} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d69e0c-b8b4-5024-b613-427b6c9b068b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360988} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb5c5a9-afc5-5b82-b112-2a1c8ebe89cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=4x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360989} {:answer/id #uuid "57cb1294-7bec-5d0b-8cc3-a949790155cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360990} {:answer/id #uuid "af0e531c-5da6-5f96-9dec-e09715f94105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2x-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0d69e0c-b8b4-5024-b613-427b6c9b068b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360988} :answer-field/id #uuid "783ebf7e-1c97-5153-b435-bf8208034b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bba97e87-cbd7-52ca-b400-b76d84b8e243" :question/math-academy-id "q-93858" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{2}}+{c}_{2}x,\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(2)=1.$ Substituting $y(2)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{4}+2{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=4-8{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=2.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{{x}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=2$ and ${c}_{1}=4-8{c}_{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-\\frac{8-16{c}_{2}}{8}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & =3{c}_{2}-1 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10cb8c3a-0764-5082-b431-7f97b8bc5782" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1c32e8a-9d38-58c5-8647-9f4894f2f33a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360994} {:answer/id #uuid "17272277-da18-506f-92e6-9345bcb16c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}+\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360995} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9a0888-1967-537d-966f-af50a4782fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{1/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360996} {:answer/id #uuid "8eec7487-9283-523c-8e17-464d042f2bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3{x}^{1/3}+\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360997} {:answer/id #uuid "d3addf56-3e0a-5394-942a-66a5b16c500d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{1/3}}{3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1c32e8a-9d38-58c5-8647-9f4894f2f33a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360994} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cff47b4-ce91-51af-b233-f1f51c2c5557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87887c3a-f4f3-5503-b32a-2d4f279bbcb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-93862" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{1/3}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{x}^{2/3}}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5{c}_{1}}{6} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{5}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$"}]}
